1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:11,275
A man is sitting in the dust beside the busiest road in Judea, and he hears
something change. It's not the usual traffic.

2
00:00:11,325 --> 00:00:25,060
It's not the mule trains and the pilgrims and the tax men. No, it's a crowd. A big
one. And it's moving as one thing. But there's a sound inside it he cannot place.

3
00:00:25,110 --> 00:00:38,034
He has sat by this road for years so he knows every sound it makes, but he can't
quite place this one. He knows it is something he has not heard before.

4
00:00:38,084 --> 00:00:43,710
So he does what blind people do so many times each day. He asks.

5
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:56,239
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

6
00:00:56,289 --> 00:01:05,189
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

7
00:01:05,239 --> 00:01:09,470
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

8
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:19,823
First, where we are. It is spring, a few days before Passover, and Jesus is on His
last journey to Jerusalem.

9
00:01:19,873 --> 00:01:31,591
He has told His disciples three times that He is going there to die, and yet He
continues His work: teaching, ministering, being present in the moment.

10
00:01:31,641 --> 00:01:45,539
A large crowd travels with Him, because everyone is going up to the feast, and the
road from Galilee and from across the Jordan funnels through one city before the
final climb.

11
00:01:45,589 --> 00:01:58,179
That city is Jericho. Mark shows us the mood on that road just before Jericho.
Listen for a question Jesus asks, because He is about to ask it again.

12
00:01:58,229 --> 00:02:01,230
Mark chapter 10, verses 32 to 37.

13
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:09,559
[Reader] As they were going up the road to Jerusalem, Jesus was walking ahead of
them. The disciples were amazed, but those who followed were afraid.

14
00:02:09,609 --> 00:02:15,556
[Reader] Again Jesus took the Twelve aside and began to tell them what was going to
happen to Him:

15
00:02:15,606 --> 00:02:22,886
[Reader] “Look, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered
over to the chief priests and scribes.

16
00:02:22,936 --> 00:02:32,214
[Reader] They will condemn Him to death and will deliver Him over to the Gentiles,
who will mock Him and spit on Him and flog Him and kill Him.

17
00:02:32,264 --> 00:02:46,540
[Reader] And after three days He will rise again.” Then James and John, the sons of
Zebedee, came to Jesus and declared, “Teacher, we want You to do for us whatever we
ask.” “What do you want Me to do for you?” He inquired.

18
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:53,870
[Reader] They answered, “Grant that one of us may sit at Your right hand and the
other at Your left in Your glory.”

19
00:02:55,920 --> 00:03:07,457
What do you want Me to do for you? He asks it of James and John, who want the seats
of honour in His kingdom. Hold on to that question.

20
00:03:07,507 --> 00:03:19,430
A few verses later, outside Jericho, He will ask it of a blind beggar, in the very
same words, and get a very different answer. Three Gospels tell the beggar's story.

21
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:31,790
Mark tells it first and gives the man a name. We will hear Mark, then Luke, then
Matthew, and notice where they differ. Mark first. Mark chapter 10, verses 46 to 52.

22
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:35,900
[Reader] Next, they came to Jericho.

23
00:03:35,950 --> 00:03:47,294
[Reader] And as Jesus and His disciples were leaving Jericho with a large crowd, a
blind beggar named Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus, was sitting beside the road.

24
00:03:47,344 --> 00:04:10,505
[Reader] When he heard that it was Jesus of Nazareth, he began to cry out, “Jesus,
Son of David, have mercy on me!” Many people admonished him to be silent, but he
cried out all the louder, “Son of David, have mercy on me!” Jesus stopped and said,
“Call him.” So they called the blind man.

25
00:04:10,555 --> 00:04:19,789
[Reader] “Take courage!” they said. “Get up! He is calling for you.” Throwing off
his cloak, Bartimaeus jumped up and came to Jesus.

26
00:04:19,839 --> 00:04:24,432
[Reader] “What do you want Me to do for you?” Jesus asked.

27
00:04:24,482 --> 00:04:36,670
[Reader] “Rabboni,” said the blind man, “let me see again.” “Go,” said Jesus, “your
faith has healed you.” And immediately he received his sight and followed Jesus
along the road.

28
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:49,265
Son of Timaeus. Bar means son, so Mark has told us his name twice. He is one of very
few people Jesus healed whose name we know at all.

29
00:04:49,315 --> 00:05:00,590
Henry's guess is that Mark names him because he was a well known, much talked of
beggar, the kind of man a whole city knew by his place on the road.

30
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:10,324
The place. Jericho lies in the Jordan Valley about two hundred and fifty metres
below sea level, the lowest city in the world.

31
00:05:10,374 --> 00:05:18,092
It was warm when Jerusalem was cold, which is why Herod built palaces there and why
Jerusalem's wealthy wintered there.

32
00:05:18,142 --> 00:05:28,968
Josephus called the plain the most fruitful country in Judea, with its palms and its
balsam trees, whose sap was gathered as it dripped, he said, like tears.

33
00:05:29,018 --> 00:05:34,794
Smith's dictionary says that in Jesus's day Jericho was once more a city of palms.

34
00:05:34,844 --> 00:05:46,447
Edersheim pictures its streets at Passover full of pilgrims from Galilee and from
beyond the Jordan, priests going up to serve, traders, and the caravan traffic from
Arabia and Damascus.

35
00:05:46,497 --> 00:05:54,215
It was also the central station for collecting taxes, which is why a chief tax
collector named Zacchaeus lived there.

36
00:05:54,265 --> 00:05:58,488
Luke tells his story in the next breath after this one.

37
00:05:58,538 --> 00:06:08,198
From Jericho the road to Jerusalem climbs about a thousand metres in roughly six
hours of walking, through dry rocky hills with no town between.

38
00:06:08,248 --> 00:06:15,190
Edersheim, writing about the Good Samaritan, calls that road notoriously unsafe, the
kind of country robbers hid in.

39
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:29,950
Everyone leaving Jericho for the feast was about to start that climb together, for
safety as much as company, and the edge of the city was the last place to sit and
ask for help before the hills.

40
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:40,826
Smith's says that beggars in later times had fixed places, at street corners, at the
Temple gates, at the gates of houses, and it cites this very verse.

41
00:06:40,876 --> 00:06:49,371
People who could not work were usually cared for by relatives. A man begging by the
road may have had no one.

42
00:06:49,421 --> 00:06:59,470
Or he may have had someone who walked him to his place each morning and came back
for him at dusk. The text does not say.

43
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:11,812
Scripture does not tell us what Bartimaeus heard before he heard the name, or who
answered him. We imagine a man who knew his road by ear.

44
00:07:11,862 --> 00:07:14,110
This is a scene, not Scripture.

45
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:22,089
[Bartimaeus] Hooves. A cart with one wheel that squeaks, the same cart every
morning. Sandals, hundreds of them, going up. Pilgrims.

46
00:07:22,139 --> 00:07:33,660
[Bartimaeus] They talk about the feast and about the price of lambs. But this is
different. This crowd is slower, and it is turned inward, like people walking around
something.

47
00:07:33,710 --> 00:07:45,230
[Bartimaeus] And under the talk there is one voice they are all listening to.
Friend. You, with the basket, I can hear it creak. What is this? Who is passing?

48
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:52,030
[Voice] Jesus. The Nazarene. The teacher who healed in Galilee. He is going up to
the feast.

49
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:56,270
[Bartimaeus] Jesus. Son of David. Son of David, have mercy on me!

50
00:07:56,320 --> 00:08:00,190
[Voice] Quiet. He is teaching. You will not be heard over this crowd anyway.

51
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,910
[Bartimaeus] Son of David! Have mercy on me!

52
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:14,270
[Bartimaeus] The crowd stops. I hear it stop, the way a river sound stops when you
put your hand over your ears. Feet shuffling. Someone far ahead has said something.

53
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:19,390
[Voice] Take courage! Get up, he is calling you! Here, your hand, this way.

54
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:26,250
[Bartimaeus] My cloak is on the ground with the morning's coins in it. Leave it.
Leave all of it.

55
00:08:27,800 --> 00:08:39,403
Then the question. Jesus has a blind man standing in front of Him, and He asks what
he wants. The same words He had just asked two disciples.

56
00:08:39,453 --> 00:08:43,564
James and John asked for glory. Bartimaeus asked to see.

57
00:08:43,614 --> 00:08:56,881
Henry says that although Christ knows our needs, He wants to hear them from us, and
that having to say the thing plainly teaches us the worth of what we ask for.

58
00:08:56,931 --> 00:09:08,950
Edersheim notices what came after: the man did not go home. He followed Jesus on the
way, glorifying God, and all the people praised God when they saw it.

59
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:21,175
A word about the voice you are about to hear. Brian decided he was too shy to record
his own thoughts. We suspect he was being a scaredy cat.

60
00:09:21,225 --> 00:09:29,990
So, against our better judgment, we let him pick his own voice. Whenever you hear
it, from here to the end, these are his words.

61
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:40,875
[Brian] Bartimaeus asks to see again. That one word is the whole difference between
him and the man born blind. I lost my sight in 2014, after a lifetime of seeing.

62
00:09:40,925 --> 00:09:48,784
[Brian] I know what a face looks like, what Jericho's palms would have looked like,
what a road looks like going uphill into the distance.

63
00:09:48,834 --> 00:09:57,681
[Brian] The man born blind had nothing to compare the dark to. Bartimaeus did. Every
day he sat by that road he knew exactly what he was missing.

64
00:09:57,731 --> 00:10:02,623
[Brian] He hears a crowd and has to ask what it is. I do that constantly.

65
00:10:02,673 --> 00:10:12,179
[Brian] A room goes quiet, or everyone laughs, or chairs scrape, and I have to ask
the person next to me what just happened. Most of the time people answer.

66
00:10:12,229 --> 00:10:21,406
[Brian] Sometimes they are too busy to. Bartimaeus got an answer, and it was the
most important sentence anyone ever said to him. People told him to be quiet.

67
00:10:21,456 --> 00:10:30,962
[Brian] If you are blind and loud in public, people get uncomfortable, because you
are the one making a scene and they cannot make eye contact to settle you down.

68
00:10:31,012 --> 00:10:32,939
[Brian] He shouted louder. I love that.

69
00:10:32,989 --> 00:10:42,824
[Brian] Then, the moment Jesus noticed him, he suddenly got encouragement from the
crowd when they shouted for him to take courage, he is calling you. The crowd did
not change.

70
00:10:42,874 --> 00:10:45,790
[Brian] His standing in it did. Jesus lifts everyone up.

71
00:10:47,340 --> 00:10:56,090
Luke tells the same story, with one difference you will hear right at the start.
Luke chapter 18, verses 35 to 43.

72
00:10:57,140 --> 00:11:07,766
[Reader] As Jesus drew near to Jericho, a blind man was sitting beside the road,
begging. When he heard the crowd going by, he asked what was happening.

73
00:11:07,816 --> 00:11:11,324
[Reader] “Jesus of Nazareth is passing by,” they told him.

74
00:11:11,374 --> 00:11:29,908
[Reader] So he called out, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” Those who led
the way admonished him to be silent, but he cried out all the louder, “Son of David,
have mercy on me!” Jesus stopped and directed that the man be brought to Him.

75
00:11:29,958 --> 00:11:41,374
[Reader] When he had come near, Jesus asked him, “What do you want Me to do for
you?” “Lord,” he said, “let me see again.” “Receive your sight!” Jesus replied.

76
00:11:41,424 --> 00:11:52,050
[Reader] “Your faith has healed you.” Immediately he received his sight and followed
Jesus, glorifying God. And all the people, when they saw it, gave praise to God.

77
00:11:54,100 --> 00:12:03,188
Luke says as Jesus drew near to Jericho. Mark says as He was leaving. There were in
fact two Jerichos in Jesus's day:

78
00:12:03,238 --> 00:12:15,107
the old city of Joshua's time lay in ruins near Elisha's spring, and the new town
Herod and his son built, with palaces and gardens, stood a short distance off.

79
00:12:15,157 --> 00:12:25,040
Some readers since have used that to make the accounts agree, with the healing on
the road between them. None of our old books does.

80
00:12:25,090 --> 00:12:31,397
Matthew Henry says only that Luke's word for near can mean leaving as well as
arriving.

81
00:12:31,447 --> 00:12:43,713
Edersheim says plainly that it is better to admit the difficulty than to force a
harmony. We will leave it where he left it. Notice what Luke adds at the end.

82
00:12:43,763 --> 00:12:56,427
The man follows, glorifying God, and all the people, the same people who told him to
be quiet, give praise to God. The crowd that shushed him ends up shouting with him.

83
00:12:56,477 --> 00:13:05,168
And Luke is not finished with Jericho. In his telling, Jesus has just reached the
edge of the city when Bartimaeus shouts.

84
00:13:05,218 --> 00:13:16,690
Now He walks on into it, with the healed man somewhere in the crowd behind Him, and
stops again. Read straight on. Luke chapter 19, verses 1 to 11.

85
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:29,218
[Reader] Then Jesus entered Jericho and was passing through. And there was a man
named Zacchaeus, a chief tax collector, who was very wealthy.

86
00:13:29,268 --> 00:13:37,004
[Reader] He was trying to see who Jesus was, but could not see over the crowd
because he was small in stature.

87
00:13:37,054 --> 00:13:44,790
[Reader] So he ran on ahead and climbed a sycamore tree to see Him, since Jesus was
about to pass that way.

88
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:55,913
[Reader] When Jesus came to that place, He looked up and said, “Zacchaeus, hurry
down, for I must stay at your house today.” So Zacchaeus hurried down and welcomed
Him joyfully.

89
00:13:55,963 --> 00:14:22,238
[Reader] And all who saw this began to grumble, saying, “He has gone to be the guest
of a sinful man!” But Zacchaeus stood up and said to the Lord, “Look, Lord, half of
my possessions I give to the poor, and if I have cheated anyone, I will repay it
fourfold.” Jesus said to him, “Today salvation has come to this house, because this
man too is a son of Abraham.

90
00:14:22,288 --> 00:14:37,810
[Reader] For the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.” While the people
were listening to this, Jesus proceeded to tell them a parable, because He was near
Jerusalem and they thought the kingdom of God would appear imminently.

91
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:47,101
Two stops in one town. Put them side by side. Bartimaeus could not see Jesus because
of his eyes.

92
00:14:47,151 --> 00:14:57,353
Zacchaeus could not see Him because of the crowd, and because he was short. Both of
them were blocked by the same crowd of pilgrims.

93
00:14:57,403 --> 00:15:10,885
Both did something a grown man did not do in public in that world: one shouted a
Messianic title at the top of his lungs, the other ran ahead and climbed a tree.

94
00:15:10,935 --> 00:15:19,906
And Jesus stopped for both, and called each of them by what he was. The beggar had
called Him Son of David.

95
00:15:19,956 --> 00:15:29,338
Jesus called the tax collector a son of Abraham, the one name the whole town had
taken away from him. The place fits.

96
00:15:29,388 --> 00:15:41,230
Edersheim says Jericho was the tax station for the Jordan crossing and for the
balsam trade, which is why a chief tax collector was rich there and hated there.

97
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:51,481
Sycamore figs lined the roads of the plain; the old dictionaries describe them as
low-branched, spreading trees, the easiest tree in the country to climb.

98
00:15:51,531 --> 00:16:04,603
A man who wanted to see over a crowd could not have chosen better. Then the line
that ends the story, and names what Jesus had been doing all day in Jericho:

99
00:16:04,653 --> 00:16:12,395
the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost. And look at the verse after it.

100
00:16:12,445 --> 00:16:25,517
Luke says Jesus went on to tell a parable because He was near Jerusalem and the
people thought the kingdom was about to appear. He is teaching with the city in
sight.

101
00:16:25,567 --> 00:16:41,509
He has told His disciples three times what waits for Him there, and on the last road
up to it He heals a beggar, dines with a tax collector, and stops to teach a crowd
who have misunderstood Him.

102
00:16:41,559 --> 00:16:46,430
The cross is days away and He is not hurrying past anyone.

103
00:16:50,980 --> 00:16:59,735
[Brian] Here is what gets me about Jericho. Jesus knew exactly where He was going
and exactly what was waiting for Him. He had said it out loud three times.

104
00:16:59,785 --> 00:17:06,414
[Brian] Most of us, with that ahead of us, would have put our heads down and walked.
He stopped twice in one town.

105
00:17:06,464 --> 00:17:15,826
[Brian] Once for a beggar who was shouting, and once for a little tax man up a tree
that the whole city despised. Neither of them was on the way to anything.

106
00:17:15,876 --> 00:17:24,326
[Brian] He was not teaching a crowd or healing a hundred people. He was being
present for one person at a time, with the cross a few days off.

107
00:17:24,376 --> 00:17:30,399
[Brian] That is the Jesus I want to follow, and honestly, it is the Jesus I want to
be more like.

108
00:17:30,449 --> 00:17:39,507
[Brian] I get busy and I get focused on where I am headed, and the man shouting by
the road becomes an interruption. He never once treated anyone as an interruption.

109
00:17:39,557 --> 00:17:49,222
[Brian] Bartimaeus and Zacchaeus could not see Him, and for opposite reasons. One
had no eyes for it. One had a crowd in the way and was too short to see over it.

110
00:17:49,272 --> 00:17:52,865
[Brian] I know both of those. I know the first one every day.

111
00:17:52,915 --> 00:18:03,795
[Brian] And I know the second one from every church lobby and conference hall where
the crowd between me and the person I want to reach is just people, standing,
talking, not unkind, just in the way.

112
00:18:03,845 --> 00:18:09,867
[Brian] Bartimaeus shouted. Zacchaeus climbed. Both actions are not something a
grown man is supposed to do in public, but hey.

113
00:18:09,917 --> 00:18:16,850
[Brian] If they get to do it, watch out Tom and Treesa, and anyone else in the pews
in front of me at church.

114
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:28,150
Matthew tells it last, and shortest, and he counts differently. Matthew chapter 20,
verses 29 to 34.

115
00:18:28,950 --> 00:18:37,371
[Reader] As they were leaving Jericho, a large crowd followed Him. And there were
two blind men sitting beside the road.

116
00:18:37,421 --> 00:18:56,853
[Reader] When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, “Lord, Son of
David, have mercy on us!” The crowd admonished them to be silent, but they cried out
all the louder, “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!” Jesus stopped and called
them.

117
00:18:56,903 --> 00:19:01,512
[Reader] “What do you want Me to do for you?” He asked.

118
00:19:01,562 --> 00:19:12,100
[Reader] “Lord,” they answered, “let our eyes be opened.” Moved with compassion,
Jesus touched their eyes, and at once they received their sight and followed Him.

119
00:19:12,650 --> 00:19:17,428
Two blind men. Mark and Luke have one, and Mark names him.

120
00:19:17,478 --> 00:19:29,096
Edersheim suggests Bartimaeus was the spokesman of two, the one whose voice carried
and whose name was remembered. That is a reasonable guess and it is only a guess.

121
00:19:29,146 --> 00:19:40,763
What all three agree on is the shout, the crowd telling them to be quiet, Jesus
stopping, and the question. Matthew also adds a detail the others leave out.

122
00:19:40,813 --> 00:19:54,040
Jesus touched their eyes. In Mark and Luke the healing is by a word alone. Your
faith has healed you. Receive your sight. In Matthew there is a hand on the face
first.

123
00:19:55,590 --> 00:20:02,758
[Brian] He threw off his cloak. For a beggar that cloak was his bed, his coat, and
the thing he spread out to collect coins.

124
00:20:02,808 --> 00:20:11,179
[Brian] He left it in the dust because he was not planning to come back to that
spot. My white cane is the thing I would never leave behind.

125
00:20:11,229 --> 00:20:21,405
[Brian] So, for me, there would be a little pile of beat up white canes, and an
iPhone. Yes, I would go back to Android. Jesus asks him what he wants. He does not
assume.

126
00:20:21,455 --> 00:20:30,427
[Brian] Every blind person I know has a story about someone who grabbed an arm and
steered them somewhere they did not want to go. People mean well, but often assume.

127
00:20:30,477 --> 00:20:35,540
[Brian] Jesus had a blind man standing in front of Him and still asked. Self-
advocacy at its finest.

128
00:20:36,590 --> 00:20:45,680
One more thing about the word. Bartimaeus asks to see again, and Luke's man asks the
same, to receive his sight back.

129
00:20:45,730 --> 00:20:58,559
The man born blind, in our first episode, had never seen at all. Scripture does not
tell us how Bartimaeus lost his sight, or when, or how much he had left.

130
00:20:58,609 --> 00:21:10,192
He may have seen light and shadow. He may have seen nothing. Blindness then, as now,
was a range, and the Bible's one word covers all of it.

131
00:21:10,242 --> 00:21:25,980
What the text does tell us is that he knew what he had lost, and that when he could
see again the first thing he chose to look at was the road to Jerusalem, following
Jesus up it.

132
00:21:28,830 --> 00:21:41,504
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at one eye
biney dot github dot i o, slash blindness in the Bible.

133
00:21:41,554 --> 00:21:53,320
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

134
00:21:53,370 --> 00:22:01,500
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

